emory across a 256-bit bus at 28 Gbps, delivering roughly 896 GB/s of memory bandwidth. The card ships with a factory overclock, hitting a 2482 MHz boost clock (about 1% above reference) and reaching up to 2497 MHz in MSI Center\'s Extreme Performance mode.</p><p>What sets this card apart from the standard Ventus 3X OC is MSI\'s Project Zero design. Rather than routing power cables over the top of the card, the 12VHPWR connection is moved to a rear-facing dongle behind a removable magnetic backplate, keeping the front of the card free of visible cabling for a cleaner build. The card still conforms to NVIDIA\'s SFF (small form factor) specifications, making it a strong fit for compact cases that prioritize both airflow and aesthetics.</p><p>Blackwell brings fourth-gen RT Cores and fifth-gen Tensor Cores to the table, enabling DLSS 4 with Multi Frame Generation, enhanced Ray Reconstruction, and Super Resolution, along with NVIDIA Reflex 2 for reduced latency. Display output includes DisplayPort 2.1b and HDMI 2.1b, supporting up to 4K at 480Hz or 8K at 120Hz with DSC, VRR, and HDR.</p><p>As part of MSI\'s Ventus line, the card keeps a neutral, understated aesthetic without RGB lighting, letting it blend into any build. Cooling comes from triple TORX Fan 5.0 blades, a copper heatsink baseplate, and square-profile core heat pipes that directly contact the baseplate for efficient heat transfer, plus a vented backplate to aid airflow. MSI\'s ZERO FROZR feature shuts the fans off entirely at low temperatures for silent idle operation, and the card is backed by MSI Center for monitoring and tuning plus MSI Afterburner for advanced overclocking control.</p><h3><strong>Specifications</strong></h3><figure class=\"table\"><table><thead><tr><th><strong>Category</strong></th><th><strong>Feature</strong></th><th><strong>Specification</strong></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Core</strong></td><td>GPU</td><td>NVIDIA GeForce RTX 5070 Ti</td></tr><tr><td>Core</td><td>Architectu