ctively a 3-slot-wide design, but keeps the same clean, adornment-free aesthetic apart from GeForce RTX branding and an Expert badge protecting the power connector during shipping.</p><p>Blackwell brings fourth-gen RT Cores and fifth-gen Tensor Cores to the table, enabling DLSS 4 with Multi Frame Generation, enhanced Ray Reconstruction, and Super Resolution. The RTX 5080 sits at roughly 85% of the RTX 5090\'s performance at a notably lower price point, offering meaningful raster and ray tracing gains over the previous-generation RTX 4080, along with a fifth-generation NVENC encoder for efficient AV1 encoding that benefits video editors working in Premiere Pro, DaVinci Resolve, and similar tools.</p><p>Cooling comes from MSI\'s Flow Frozr 2 thermal design, featuring a large vapor chamber affixed directly to the GPU and memory, connected to a heatsink spanning nearly the full length of the card via square-shaped Core Pipes. Dual ball-bearing STORMFORCE fans with seven blades handle push-pull airflow — the front fan blows air down directly onto the GPU and memory, while the rear fan pulls air through the heatsink in a pass-through design similar to NVIDIA\'s own reference cooler. The card also features DrMOS power circuitry and a PCB MSI describes as engineered with hardened circuits and optimized trace routing for reliability. Dual BIOS lets users toggle between full-performance Gaming mode and a quieter Silent mode, and the card is managed through MSI Center and MSI Afterburner for real-time monitoring, tuning, and overclocking control.</p><h3><strong>Specifications</strong></h3><figure class=\"table\"><table><thead><tr><th><strong>Category</strong></th><th><strong>Feature</strong></th><th><strong>Specification</strong></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Core</strong></td><td>GPU</td><td>NVIDIA GeForce RTX 5080</td></tr><tr><td>Core</td><td>Architecture</td><td>NVIDIA Blackwell</td></tr><tr><td>Core</td><td>CUDA Cores</td><td>10752 Units</td></tr><tr><td>Core</t